    },
    "grade": "9-10",
    "points": 6,
    "blankPoints": null,
    "wrongPoints": null,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "The diagram shows a triangle $FHI$, and a point $G$ on $FH$ such that $GH = FI$. The points $M$ and $N$ are the midpoints of $FG$ and $HI$ respectively. Angle $NMH = \\alpha^\\circ$. Which of the following gives an expression for $\\angle IFH$?",
    "image": "qimages/ukmt/pink-2017/q25.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$2\\alpha^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$(90-\\alpha)^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$45+\\alpha^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$(90-\\frac{1}{2}\\alpha)^\\circ$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$60^\\circ$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Use midlines",
        "body": "Let $P$ be the midpoint of $GI$. In triangle $FGI$, $MP\\parallel FI$ and $MP=\\tfrac12FI$. In triangle $GHI$, $PN\\parallel GH$ and $PN=\\tfrac12GH$. Since $GH=FI$, $MP=PN$, so triangle $PMN$ is isosceles with $\\angle PMN=\\angle PNM=\\angle NMH=\\alpha$ (the last because $PN\\parallel GH$). Because $MP\\parallel FI$, $\\angle IFH=\\angle PMG=\\angle PMN+\\angle NMG=2\\alpha$.\n\n**Answer: (A) $2\\alpha^\\circ$**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry"
    ],
    "difficulty": 3,
